-- The (4, 1, 4) category, e⁴ = a⁴ + b⁴ + c⁴ + d⁴, starting with only its
-- elliptic-curve families (migration 0030): all 207 published primitive
-- solutions with e = a + k³(b + c + d) for a rational k, read 2026-10-05.
-- 144 have k = 1 (the Jacobi–Madden equation) and 63 cover 26 other k. The
-- full category (Eugene Go's 45,866 solutions with e ≤ 10^7) is a later
-- import. Purely additive.
--
-- The solutions were collected from every revision of the posts linked
-- below and from Seiji Tomita's tables, verified with exact BigInt
-- arithmetic, and deduplicated. family_k is the k found by
-- src/lib/elliptic-family.ts; it matches the k each source states. Terms
-- above 2^53 − 1 are JSON strings (src/lib/terms.ts).
--
-- Credit and dates:
--   * Solutions in Tomita's table (dioph224e) take its discoverer, dated by
--     the most precise record: Tomita's dated additions per curve m
--     (dioph169e, 2015-08 and 2017-07; m = 211/150 is first cited on MO on
--     2015-07-19), Jacobi's 2010-04-11 letter (dioph63e), Rouse's MO answer
--     (2014-12-23), MacLeod's arXiv:1704.03200 (2017-04-11) and the
--     Jacobi–Madden paper (Amer. Math. Monthly, March 2008). Brudno (1964),
--     Wroblewski (2005) and Tomita's 2009 solutions are known by year only and
--     use 1 January.
--   * Other k = 1 solutions take the discoverer named in Piezas' list
--     (math.stackexchange.com/a/4865487), or the author of the post where they
--     first appear, dated by the first revision that contains them.
--   * k ≠ 1 solutions with e ≤ 10^7 lie in Go's exhaustive table, completed
--     2026-08-02, and are credited to him. The rest are credited to
--     Matej Veselovac (Vepir), dated by their first appearance in his posts.
INSERT INTO categories (id, exponent, left_count, right_count, format, notation)
VALUES ('4-1-4', 4, 1, 4, 'equality', NULL);

